.TH KOBRA-LAN 1 "2026-11-04" "Kobra LAN CLI" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kobra-lan \- experimental direct Anycubic Kobra X LAN client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kobra-lan
.RI [ global-options ] " command " [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Inspect G-code locally and perform explicitly reviewed LAN operations with JSON
output. Upload and print start require separate reviewed execution flags.
Use the help for each command before attempting an operation.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --help
Show commands and options. Each command also accepts --help.
.TP
.B --version
Show the application version.
.TP
.BI --config " path"
Select an explicit private configuration; conflicts with --printer.
.TP
.BI --printer " name"
Select a named local profile for this command.
.TP
.BI --state-dir " path"
Select durable state (default ~/.local/share/kobra-lan/uploads).
.SH PAIRING
.B kobra-lan printer pair --host 192.0.2.10
provisions an already LAN-enabled Kobra X, model 20030 / firmware 2.0.2.2.
It queries status without uploading or controlling the printer.
HTTP provisioning is unauthenticated and requires a trusted network even with
--trust-sha256; the fingerprint protects only the later MQTT connection.
On that network, review the observed
TLS fingerprint and explicitly enter y or yes on a terminal; empty input refuses.
For unattended setup, supply --trust-sha256 followed by an independently obtained
64-hex-digit server certificate fingerprint. Mismatch fails without a prompt.
The TLS handshake exchanges the client certificate; MQTT credentials are sent
only after checking the accepted server pin on the subsequent connection.
.PP
Use global --config before printer to choose an alternate absent destination.
Existing configs, including symlinks, are refused before network access.
The parent must be user-owned, nonsymlink and mode 0700; missing directories are
created privately. Generated PEMs use mode 0600 in a private .pair-ID directory.
The config is published atomically after verification without overwriting.
Retain referenced PEMs if a persistence error leaves a visible config.
Removing an isolated config and only its unreferenced PEM directory does not
revoke access on the printer. Never remove operational attempt markers.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 indicates success; 2 indicates protocol/validation failure; 3 indicates
configuration/network failure. Operations are never retried automatically.
.SH FILES
Configuration and state belong to the invoking user. Removing or purging the
package preserves them. Do not delete attempt markers to retry uncertain actions.
.SH SEE ALSO
Installed README.md, NOTICE.md and releases.md in /usr/share/doc/kobra-lan-cli/.
.SH SAVED PRINTER PROFILES
.B printer list
lists local profiles without contacting printers.
.B printer info [name]
shows saved safe metadata.
.B printer use name
selects a locally loadable default without network access.
Global
.B --printer name
and
.B --config path
are mutually exclusive and precede the command. Explicit selection overrides
the saved default. Before default selection, legacy config remains the fallback.
Broken saved defaults never fall back to another printer. Local inspect and
upload dry runs require no configured printer.
.B printer pair --name name --host IPv4 [--default]
creates a new named profile after explicit trust and qualified fresh status.
Omitting --default preserves the prior selection. Named pairing rejects --config.
.B printer remove name [--yes]
requires terminal consent or --yes, detaches registration before owned-file
cleanup and never revokes credentials or removes operational markers. Removing
the default clears implicit selection, including legacy fallback. Receipts list
deleted and preserved files. Shared, external and ambiguous files are retained.
.SH CONNECTION DIAGNOSIS
.B doctor
uses ordinary target selection and reports configuration, dependencies, HTTP TCP,
MQTT TCP, pinned TLS, MQTT authentication, fresh status and compatibility in order.
Each network stage has at most 12 seconds, sharing a 60-second network deadline.
No retries, provisioning, repairs, HTTP requests or printer controls occur.
HTTP TCP failure does not block MQTT. TCP success is not protocol qualification.
A printing target can pass. Only matching model 20030 / firmware 2.0.2.2 is qualified.
The version 1 JSON result contains target, ordered checks and healthy, including
on failure; check fields are stage, status, code, message and nullable next_step.
Exit 0 means healthy; 2 means selection/trust/protocol/compatibility failure;
3 means config/dependency/I/O/network failure and takes precedence when mixed.
Preserve trust and markers; follow safe next steps instead of pairing again.
